{"authored" [{"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "c051186d7c8fff3106f511a71105516c018c45a15663b739b60b2d61eeeb1006" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "c051186d7c8fff3106f511a71105516c018c45a15663b739b60b2d61eeeb1006" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "9b315a82feab1886a14fe30608a7c61e76937596abfcbbca7cd0b54946ad9aca" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "9b315a82feab1886a14fe30608a7c61e76937596abfcbbca7cd0b54946ad9aca" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "ad968429e0e578f1412935b842cb4ebbe7c9dd2bc2e5e7fdfd70aba9eff81b3c" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "ad968429e0e578f1412935b842cb4ebbe7c9dd2bc2e5e7fdfd70aba9eff81b3c" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/