45f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5a028284d29850a9561a589c0e9fbf85e2e10e4e7a23d4ab46a765f492955b4f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-106404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/7540559d86e836d869173da11ca0f7befba8ead07c844be1c9708591fb372042.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-79280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-106715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-79375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+(y+1)^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+(y+1)^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-79375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "e20cb2359147782169b265dacaa48431c73458345f7b891a62b3d1b4b2a6c11e"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "ab5cfb3d0f81954f0e0ca32e6972707fca6b55752fa87d00168b9f1666291d7b"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "ab5cfb3d0f81954f0e0ca32e6972707fca6b55752fa87d00168b9f1666291d7b"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "e9db4b7f735b50ef05e090421ddcd44108502586f9ecb79b27f2d3b4a26ab7b8"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "e9db4b7f735b50ef05e090421ddcd44108502586f9ecb79b27f2d3b4a26ab7b8"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "487df34167da628a14eb17b8ca6d6f2a252491f7e37f3047c9f575f067df28ca"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "487df34167da628a14eb17b8ca6d6f2a252491f7e37f3047c9f575f067df28ca"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297"}] "usage" {"e-6814" [] "q-106405" [] "e-6818" [] "q-79375" [] "q-15856" [] "e-6816" [] "e-6815" []} "basis" 1470 "retractions" [[17592186152357 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-15856.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7, 0)$?"] [17592186152725 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$x = 4\\cos \\theta  - 1, y = \\sin \\theta  + 1, 0 \\le \\theta  < 2\\pi $."] [17592186151916 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)"] [17592186151918 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)"] [17592186151919 :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)"] [17592186151919 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186149077 17592186149078 17592186149079 17592186149080 17592186149081 17592186151261 17592186151262 17592186151263 17592186151264 17592186151265 17592186293439 17592186293440 17592186293441 17592186293442 17592186293443] "immutable_fields" [[17592186149076 :answer-field/correct] [17592186149076 :answer-field/id] [17592186149076 :answer-field/key] [17592186149076 :answer-field/type] [17592186151260 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151260 :answer-field/id] [17592186151260 :answer-field/key] [17592186151260 :answer-field/type] [17592186293438 :answer-field/correct] [17592186293438 :answer-field/id] [17592186293438 :answer-field/key] [17592186293438 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
